thread_text:
maria: fyi support case #4582 - customer reports intermittent SSE streaming disconnects from the JS SDK in-browser. They see partial outputs (first ~70% of tokens) and then the connection closes; final 'done' event never arrives. Happens across Chrome and Safari. They pasted browser console: 'StreamError: unexpected EOF while parsing event'.

leo: is this only browser EventSource or does Node (server-side rendering) see the same? any 4xx/5xx around the close?

maria: customer says both. Node server that proxies to our API logs show 200 responses then abrupt socket close. No 429s in their app logs but we can see 499s at the nginx front-line in our edge logs for a subset of requests. Sample edge log snippet they attached: `
[edge] 2026-02-17T18:42:12Z GET /v1/streams 200 chunked close_by_peer
[edge] 2026-02-17T18:42:12Z GET /v1/streams 499 client_closed_request
`

oliver: thanks. on the SDK side we've got a known SSE parser edge-case where chunk boundaries split CRLF sequences and the parser mistakenly treats it as end-of-stream. We patched it in canary last month but didn't backport to stable. Are they on @redwood/sdks-js v1.9.2 (stable) or canary?

maria: they're using v1.9.2 stable in prod.

vikram: quick note from runtime: we rolled a small change that flushes chunks earlier to reduce latency (micro-flush) on 2026-02-10. That increases # of tiny chunks which can expose client parsers that assume event boundaries align with chunks. Can reproduce locally with smaller chunk sizes. I'll toggle the rollout if this looks like the cause.

maria: can someone post a minimal repro? customer gave a curl snippet but it's behind auth so hard to share.

oliver: repro you can run locally pointing at our hosted API (use a test key). Example curl to see raw SSE chunking:

```
curl -v -N -H "Authorization: Bearer $REDWOOD_KEY" \
  "https://api.redwood.example/v1/streams?model=redwood-alpha&stream=true"
```

then watch bytes; if you see events split with CR and LF in separate chunks that's the failure mode. Also a quick JS workaround (for browsers) if they can't upgrade to canary: avoid EventSource wrapper and use fetch + ReadableStream + incremental decoder to reassemble partial lines. Example:

```
// fetch-based SSE-ish reader for robustness
const resp = await fetch(url, { headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${key}` } });
const reader = resp.body.getReader();
const decoder = new TextDecoder();
let buf = '';
while (true) {
  const { value, done } = await reader.read();
  if (done) break;
  buf += decoder.decode(value, { stream: true });
  let idx;
  while ((idx = buf.indexOf('\n\n')) !== -1) {
    const event = buf.slice(0, idx).trim();
    buf = buf.slice(idx + 2);
    // handle event (could be 'data: ...' lines)
  }
}
```

Note: this avoids chunk-boundary assumptions.

leo: good. are we seeing duplicate tokens on reconnect? customer also reported seeing the last 10 tokens repeated when reconnecting. could be resume logic.

vikram: by design our service currently treats a reconnect as a new request unless client sends a resume token header. But our retry/proxy layer has a subtle case: if upstream finishes sending final 'done' event right as the proxy reconnects, we can surface both old final chunk and restart stream which looks like duplicates. That's a bug in the proxy dedupe window. I'll schedule a fix to coalesce final 'done' emissions for identical request fingerprints.

maria: customer also worried about being charged for repeated partial streams. can support credit them if confirmed duplicates?

leo: yes — we'll open billing investigation and apply a credit while we triage. note that if there are many reconnects it's possible usage counts multiple attempts; we'll audit per-request ids to avoid over-crediting.

oliver: on SDK side we'll cut a patch to stable to handle split CRLF edge cases and add tests that simulate tiny chunk sizes. also add a guard in the JS event emitter to dedupe final 'done' emission for the same request id (best-effort client-side mitigation). I'll post a PR to stable and canary PR numbers when ready.

maria: i also see a Python async iterator regression reported by another customer: when using the async generator (.stream()) they see StopAsyncIteration raised prematurely. might be same root cause (chunk splits -> parser loop exits). oliver: please coordinate with python sdk owner to backport similar fix.

vikram: plan from runtime: 1) temporarily rollback micro-flush change for hosted region eu-west-1 where most reports are, 2) add dedupe window for final event emission in proxy, 3) add metrics to count 'partial-close' vs 'clean-done' so we can quantify. ETA: rollback + metric in 2h, dedupe patch in next 48h.

maria: action items: (1) support to repro with customer on canary + fetch workaround and grant them a temporary test key (maria) ; (2) billing to open credit hold and audit (leo) ; (3) runtime rollback & dedupe (vikram) ; (4) SDK patches for JS + Python to stable channels (oliver). I'm pinging @deploy-bot to mark this as P1 to track.

deploy-bot: rollback of micro-flush completed for eu-west-1 at 2026-02-17T21:05:23Z by rollout-controller.

maria: thanks everyone — i'll update the customer and keep this thread live until patches land and billing resolved. if you want logs from the customer's Node proxy i can paste sample request ids.
